{
	"name": "Heap Sort",
	"summary": "Turns the array into a max heap, then keeps moving the largest element to the end.",
	"description": [
		"Heap Sort looks at the array as a binary tree: the children of the element at index i are at 2i + 1 and 2i + 2. In a max heap, every parent is at least as large as its children, so the largest element is always the root, arr[0].",
		"First, the array is turned into a max heap from the bottom up. Every element that has children is sifted down: it is swapped with its larger child until both children are smaller. Then the root, the largest element, is swapped with the last element of the heap. The heap shrinks by one, the swapped element is in its final place, and the new root is sifted down. This repeats until the heap is empty.",
		"Building the heap takes O(n) time and every one of the n − 1 extractions sifts down at most log n levels, so Heap Sort takes O(n log n) time in every case. Like Quick Sort it sorts in place, but unlike Quick Sort it has no bad inputs. It is not stable, because the swaps move elements across long distances."
	],
